From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenBook <noreply@anybook.invalid>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: SystemUI: Quire's halo, and the pointer and window
 for contextual search
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

OpenBook's agent layer, Quire (docs/quire-design.md §2), answers a call in
its first 100 ms with a halo at the pointer and an outline around the window
it takes in, and opens its note beside the pointer. The contextual cursor has
no feedback layer and passes contextual search no position: the shake calls
invokeContextualSearch(ENTRYPOINT_SYSTEM_ACTION, config = null).

- The shake detector remembers the last pointer position (QuirePointer).
- On a shake, SystemUI finds the topmost visible app task under the pointer
  (never the home task, which spans the display beneath every window) and
  passes a ContextualSearchConfig with the display, the task's bounds as
  sourceBounds, and in intentExtras the pointer's position, the task's
  package and the time the bounds were read, which
  ContextualSearchManagerService copies onto the launch intent. Over the
  desktop there are no bounds and Quire takes in nothing. Then it shows
  the halo (QuireHalo): a 32 dp conic ring that blooms with a spring, the
  intake outline and a label, gone 600 ms later. It is a trusted, untouchable,
  unfocusable overlay; with Remove animations nothing moves.
- AssistManager.startAssist adds the same position, bounds, package and
  time to the assistant's args, with the halo, when the assistant is Quire
  and the pointer moved in the last 10 s (Meta+Space, the assist key).
  Otherwise it adds nothing, and Quire takes in nothing.

Nothing changes for other contextual search apps or assistants beyond the
extra launch extras, which use Quire's own keys.
